This code checks that the last position of X in the original array is greater than or equal to the position of X in the array.

Please keep in mind that the position of a value in an array (or list) starts from 0, and that may make the assertion fail. If it's not intented to remove the last element, it would make sense to assert that after the Remove operation, the last index should not be the removed index.--------------------Sure, I can help with this. If you want to generate the `Debug.Assert` statements that correspond to the comments that start with "@@@", you can use the following regex (`(?<=// @@@ ).*`) together with a regex replace in a text editor. This regex match the comments that start with "@@@" and replaces those comment lines with the equivalent `Debug.Assert()` statement.

Here's how to do it:

```
