Please note: the logic "greater than or equal to" can lead to failures if x is not present in the array in some specific cases, as the method IndexOf(int) can return -1 in such cases. If the list doesn't contain such an element it doesn't change the array, therefore not fulfilling the condition. Nevertheless, the requirement about "The last position of x in old array is greater than or equal to" could be rewritten as "The position of x in the same array is not lower than the last position of x in old array". In that case, this line of code wouldn't return true:

```cs
Assert.IsTrue(arrList.IndexOf(x) >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```--------------------The comment starting with "@@@" states that "the last position of x in old array is greater than or equal to some position of x in the same array". To generate a corresponding assertion we need to check that the previous and post-removal 'LastIndexOf' are the same or higher for x. Because we don't know what kind of values can be in arrList, we cannot directly assert that New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX. We don't have a unique identifier for x in arrList and we don't know which element we're removing if there are multiple x in the array, so we need to use a looping mechanism or rely on some form of unique indexing for x to get the necessary information. Also as PexObserve can't be used in place of Debug.Assert, consider replacing PexObserve with Debug.Assert.

Following C# code shows how 'LastIndexOf' might be checked with a loop to make it clear which element of x we're dealing with:
```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount >= Old_arrListCount); // As the count of arrList is not decreased
int Old_count = Old_arrListCount;
int New_count = New_arrListCount;
for(int i=Old_count-1; i>=0; i--)
{
    if(arrList[i] == x)
    {
        Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= i); // For each index of x we found, make sure its last position is at least same as the previous one
        Debug.Assert(i <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX || !New_arrListContainsX); // For a removed x, it should not appear in the new collection
        break;
    }
}
```
The code above uses `Debug.Assert` to make assertions based on the analysis of the data, but the specifics might need to be adjusted based on the actual logic of your code. Make sure to test and refine this assertion with your own data after the actual execution of your test methods. 

The provided code example only contains a few values under separate variable names for readability. In a production code you would not want to have only one 'x' in the 'arrList', if 'x' is meant to be only a part of 'arrList' use an identifier for it to make sure that the unique value is the one being removed or modified.--------------------